# Skill: pr-presentation
# Scope: FuelGuard AI Backend (Node.js + Express + Supabase)
# Used by: Antigravity AI coding agent
# Reference doc: docs/FuelGuard_Backend_Spec.md
# Toolchain: Graphite (gt) for stacked PRs

---

## PURPOSE

Every PR Antigravity opens must be immediately reviewable — no guessing required.
This skill defines the exact format for PR titles, descriptions, commit messages,
stack metadata, complexity guidelines, and review readiness checks.
A PR that fails these rules is NOT ready to open — fix it first.

---

## 1. PR TITLE FORMAT

Pattern: {type}({scope}): {imperative summary under 72 chars}

### Types
  feat      → new functionality
  fix       → bug fix
  refactor  → code change with no behavior change
  test      → adding or updating tests only
  docs      → documentation only
  chore     → build config, deps, env, tooling
  db        → database schema or migration change

### Scope (matches folder or domain)
  auth, telemetry, vehicles, devices, drivers, alerts, trips,
  websocket, db, config, middleware, rules, ci

### Examples (good)
  feat(telemetry): add normalized insert and vehicle state upsert
  feat(rules): implement LOW_FUEL alert rule with dedup check
  feat(trips): open trip on engine-on transition
  feat(websocket): broadcast VEHICLE_UPDATE after telemetry ingestion
  feat(auth): add JWT login endpoint for admin
  db(schema): add driver_violations table
  test(rules): unit tests for LOW_FUEL and OVERSPEED rules
  docs(api): add Swagger spec for POST /api/telemetry
  fix(trips): prevent duplicate trip open on rapid engine flicker
  refactor(telemetry): extract resolveVehicleFromDevice to own function

### Examples (bad — reject these)
  ❌  "WIP telemetry stuff"
  ❌  "Fix bug"
  ❌  "Updates"
  ❌  "feat: added the new thing for the fuel alert system endpoint"  (too vague, no scope)
  ❌  "feat(telemetry): implement entire telemetry pipeline including trip detection, alert rules, websocket broadcast, and DB writes" (too large — decompose into PRs)

---

## 2. COMMIT MESSAGE FORMAT

Pattern: {type}({scope}): {imperative summary}

One commit per logical unit of work. Do not squash unrelated changes into one commit.

### Single-line (for small, clear changes)
  feat(db): create telemetry_raw and telemetry_normalized tables
  feat(middleware): add deviceAuthMiddleware for X-Device-Token check
  test(auth): add smoke test for POST /api/auth/login 401 case

### Multi-line (when the why needs explanation)
  fix(trips): guard against duplicate active trip on engine flicker

  The hardware sometimes toggles engine ON/OFF within a single telemetry
  window when the vehicle is idling. Without a guard, this created ghost
  trips with 0-second duration. Added a 3-second minimum gap check before
  opening a new trip when an active trip already exists.

  Relates to: FuelGuard_Backend_Spec.md §6.1

### Rules
  - Imperative mood: "add", "fix", "implement", "remove" — NOT "added", "fixed"
  - No period at the end of the summary line
  - Body wrapped at 72 characters per line
  - Reference spec section if the commit implements something from the spec

---

## 3. PR DESCRIPTION TEMPLATE

Every PR must use this exact structure. No section may be omitted.
Antigravity fills this in before opening any PR.

```markdown
## What this PR does
<!-- One paragraph. What behavior or capability does this add/change/fix? -->
<!-- Be specific: name the functions, tables, endpoints, or rules involved. -->

## Why
<!-- Which requirement from FuelGuard_Backend_Spec.md does this implement? -->
<!-- Link the spec section: e.g. "Implements spec §5.2 steps 5–6" -->

## Changes
<!-- Bullet list of files changed and what changed in each -->
- `src/services/telemetry-service.js` — added `insertTelemetryRaw()` and `insertTelemetryNormalized()`
- `src/db/schema.sql` — added `telemetry_raw` and `telemetry_normalized` table definitions
- `tests/unit/telemetry-service.test.js` — unit tests for mapping and insert functions

## Stack position (Graphite)
<!-- If this is part of a stack, state it explicitly -->
- Base branch: `main`                          ← if this is stack base
- Stacked on: `feat/telemetry-raw-insert`      ← if this depends on another PR
- Next PR in stack: `feat/vehicle-state-upsert` ← if known

## Test coverage
<!-- What tests were added or updated? What do they verify? -->
- Unit: `insertTelemetryRaw` stores correct rawPayload JSON
- Unit: `mapRawToNormalized` maps all 18 hardware fields correctly
- Smoke: POST /api/telemetry returns 200 with valid payload
- Coverage: services/telemetry-service.js → 87% line coverage

## API / Schema changes
<!-- List any new or changed endpoints, DB tables, columns, or WS events -->
<!-- If none: write "None" -->
- New table: `telemetry_raw`
- New table: `telemetry_normalized`
- No endpoint changes in this PR

## Swagger updated?
<!-- YES → name the endpoint(s) updated in swagger.yaml -->
<!-- NO  → explain why not (e.g. "no endpoint changes in this PR") -->
YES — POST /api/telemetry request/response spec added to swagger.yaml

## Documentation updated?
<!-- YES → name the doc file(s) updated -->
<!-- NO  → state "no doc changes needed" or flag if docs ARE needed but deferred -->
No doc changes needed — spec §5.2 is the source of truth for this PR.

## How to test manually
<!-- Step-by-step, from a clean state -->
1. Run `npm run db:migrate` to apply schema changes
2. Start the server: `npm run dev`
3. POST to `/api/telemetry` with the sample payload from spec §1.1 and header `X-Device-Id: ESP32-001`
4. Verify 200 response with `accepted: true`
5. Check Supabase → `telemetry_raw` table has new row
6. Check Supabase → `telemetry_normalized` table has corresponding row

## Checklist
- [ ] All tests pass locally (`npm test`)
- [ ] No new ESLint warnings
- [ ] No hardcoded threshold values (all read from thresholds.js)
- [ ] No console.log left in source (use logger or remove)
- [ ] Swagger updated if any endpoint was added or changed
- [ ] naming-conventions.skill rules followed
- [ ] code-structure.skill rules followed
- [ ] This PR does ONE thing — if it does more, split it
```

---

## 4. PR COMPLEXITY RULES

### Size limits (enforced before opening)
  Files changed:   ≤ 8 files per PR
  Lines added:     ≤ 400 lines (excluding test files and generated files)
  Lines deleted:   no limit (deletion is always welcome)
  Test files:      not counted toward the 400-line limit

### If a PR would exceed these limits → STOP
Antigravity must decompose into smaller PRs and propose the stack order.
Example decomposition for "implement telemetry ingestion":

  PR 1: feat(db): create telemetry_raw and telemetry_normalized schema
  PR 2: feat(telemetry): add raw insert and field normalization             ← stacked on PR 1
  PR 3: feat(telemetry): add vehicle state upsert and device status update  ← stacked on PR 2
  PR 4: feat(trips): integrate trip detection into telemetry pipeline       ← stacked on PR 3
  PR 5: feat(websocket): broadcast VEHICLE_UPDATE after telemetry           ← stacked on PR 4

Each PR has one clear purpose, passes tests independently, and can be reviewed in isolation.

### PR complexity self-check (run before opening)
  1. Can I describe what this PR does in one sentence? If not → split it.
  2. Does this PR touch more than 2 service domains? (e.g. alerts + trips + websocket) → split it.
  3. Are there tests for every new function? If not → add tests before opening.
  4. Does this PR include both schema changes AND application logic?
     Consider splitting DB changes into their own PR (faster, safer rollback).

---

## 5. GRAPHITE / GT STACK CONVENTIONS

### Naming the stack
Use a shared prefix for all PRs in a stack:

  feat/telemetry-01-schema
  feat/telemetry-02-raw-insert
  feat/telemetry-03-normalize
  feat/telemetry-04-state-upsert
  feat/telemetry-05-websocket-broadcast

### gt commands reference
  gt create -m "feat(telemetry): add raw insert"   # create new branch in stack
  gt modify -m "updated commit message"             # amend top commit
  gt sync                                           # rebase stack on main
  gt submit                                         # open all PRs in stack on GitHub
  gt log                                            # visualize the stack

### Stack PR description addendum
In the "Stack position" section of the description, always include:
- Which PR in the stack this is (e.g. "2 of 5")
- What the previous PR added (so reviewer has context without reading it)
- What the next PR will add

### Stack ordering rules
  - DB schema changes always come first in any stack
  - Middleware changes come before the routes that depend on them
  - Service logic comes before the route that calls it
  - Tests come in the same PR as the code they test (not a separate "test PR")
  - Swagger updates come in the same PR as the endpoint they document

---

## 6. REVIEW READINESS CHECKLIST

Antigravity runs this checklist before marking a PR ready for review.
If ANY item is NO → fix before opening.

### Code quality
  [ ] No TODO or FIXME comments unless they reference a specific follow-up PR
  [ ] No commented-out code blocks
  [ ] No hardcoded credentials, secrets, or threshold numbers
  [ ] No console.log in src/ (only in tests is acceptable)
  [ ] All functions exported from a module are documented with a one-line JSDoc comment

### Tests
  [ ] New functions have unit tests
  [ ] New endpoints have integration or smoke tests
  [ ] npm test passes with exit code 0
  [ ] Coverage for changed files is ≥ 80% line coverage

### API & Docs
  [ ] If an endpoint was added/changed: swagger.yaml is updated
  [ ] If a DB table was added/changed: schema.sql reflects the change
  [ ] If a threshold was added: it is in .env.example and thresholds.js

### Naming & Structure
  [ ] naming-conventions.skill rules followed throughout
  [ ] code-structure.skill layer rules followed (no routes touching DB, etc.)
  [ ] No new circular imports

### PR hygiene
  [ ] PR title matches the format in §1
  [ ] All sections of the description template in §3 are filled in
  [ ] PR does ONE thing (one-sentence description possible)
  [ ] Stack position declared if stacked

---

## 7. LABELS (apply to every PR)

Apply at least one label from each group:

### Type label (one of):
  type: feat | type: fix | type: refactor | type: test | type: docs | type: chore | type: db

### Domain label (one or more):
  domain: auth | domain: telemetry | domain: vehicles | domain: devices |
  domain: drivers | domain: alerts | domain: trips | domain: websocket | domain: db

### Size label (auto-applied based on §4 limits):
  size: S   → ≤ 100 lines added
  size: M   → 101–250 lines added
  size: L   → 251–400 lines added
  size: XL  → > 400 lines added → REQUIRES decomposition justification in description

### Stack label (if applicable):
  stacked   → this PR is part of a gt stack

---

## 8. WHAT NOT TO PUT IN A PR

❌  Mixing unrelated bug fixes with new features (open two separate PRs)
❌  Schema migrations + large application logic in one PR
❌  Commented-out old implementation "just in case"
❌  Partial implementations left as dead code (if it's not wired up, don't include it)
❌  Test files that only assert `expect(true).toBe(true)` (placeholder tests)
❌  PRs that depend on another open PR but don't declare the dependency in the description
❌  Changes to .env.example without a corresponding thresholds.js update
❌  swagger.yaml edits that don't match the actual implementation in that PR
